# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
#  Ponyo's Little Library — line endings and file types
#
#  This project is written on Windows and will be developed and run on
#  macOS (Phase 2 targets her Mac first). Without this file Git decides
#  line endings by guesswork per machine, and the first cross-platform
#  edit shows up as "every line changed" in a diff — which buries the
#  one line that actually changed.
#
#  The rule is the conventional one: LF in the repository, always;
#  whatever the platform needs in the working tree. Because there are no
#  commits yet, this takes effect cleanly with nothing to renormalise.
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────


# ── DEFAULT ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Normalise anything Git detects as text to LF on the way in. Files it
# detects as binary are left exactly as they are.
* text=auto eol=lf


# ── APPLICATION SOURCE — LF ──────────────────────────────────────────
# The app itself: ES modules, stylesheets, markup, data.
*.js      text eol=lf
*.mjs     text eol=lf
*.css     text eol=lf
*.html    text eol=lf
*.json    text eol=lf
*.svg     text eol=lf
*.md      text eol=lf
*.txt     text eol=lf

# Phase 2 additions, so the rule is already here when they arrive.
*.rs      text eol=lf
*.toml    text eol=lf
*.yml     text eol=lf
*.yaml    text eol=lf

# Repository metadata.
.gitattributes text eol=lf
.gitignore     text eol=lf


# ── WINDOWS SCRIPTS — CRLF, DELIBERATELY ─────────────────────────────
# These are the exception, and it is not cosmetic.
#
# A .bat file is read by cmd.exe line by line as it executes. With LF
# endings, labels and `goto` can mis-parse and the failure is confusing
# rather than loud. start-library.bat must stay CRLF.
#
# The .ps1 tooling is Windows-only by nature (System.Net.HttpListener,
# headless Edge) and CRLF is the platform convention for it. This also
# keeps them consistent with the ASCII-only, BOM-sensitive parsing that
# Windows PowerShell 5.1 already forced on this project.
*.bat     text eol=crlf
*.cmd     text eol=crlf
*.ps1     text eol=crlf
*.psm1    text eol=crlf
*.psd1    text eol=crlf


# ── HER ARTWORK AND ALL BINARIES — HANDS OFF ─────────────────────────
# `binary` is shorthand for `-text -diff -merge`: Git will never apply
# line-ending conversion, never try to produce a textual diff, and never
# attempt a line-level merge.
#
# `* text=auto` above would almost certainly classify these correctly on
# its own. They are listed explicitly anyway, because "do not modify the
# artwork" is a standing rule of this project and it should be written
# down where Git can enforce it rather than left to detection.
*.png     binary
*.jpg     binary
*.jpeg    binary
*.gif     binary
*.webp    binary
*.avif    binary
*.ico     binary
*.pdf     binary
*.woff    binary
*.woff2   binary
*.ttf     binary
*.otf     binary
*.mp3     binary
*.mp4     binary
*.zip     binary

# Phase 2 library backups. Ignored by .gitignore and must never be
# committed — but if one is ever force-added for a good reason, it is a
# zip of her library and must arrive byte-for-byte intact.
*.ponyolib binary


# ── LARGE ARTWORK: A NOTE, NOT A RULE ────────────────────────────────
# There is deliberately no `assets/ponyo/** binary` line here.
#
# A folder-wide pattern would win over the extension rules above — in
# .gitattributes the last match wins — and it would catch manifest.json
# and README.txt in that folder too, marking two genuinely useful text
# files as binary and throwing their diffs away. The `*.png binary` rule
# above already covers the artwork itself.
#
# This is also where a Git LFS rule would go, if that decision lands
# that way later. No LFS is configured now.
